# Shatter — Screens and the HUD

This file defines the five screens the game moves between, what each shows, how a
mouse and a touch contact drive the menus, the HUD drawn over live play, and the
wave banner. The keys the screens are driven by are in `specs/controls.md`, and the
run the `playing` screen carries is in `specs/progression.md`. Every piece of
screen copy below carries the name this specification gives it.

Every piece of text a screen shows is legible against whatever sits behind it at
the logical field size, `1280 x 720`. The palette, the type, and the layout of
each screen are yours.

## The screens

The game is on exactly one of five screens at a time.

| Screen | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | The main menu, and where the game opens. |
| `howto` | How to play. |
| `playing` | Live play. |
| `paused` | The pause menu, over the frozen field. |
| `gameover` | The game was lost. |

### `title`

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `SHATTER` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `GRAVITY WELL SHOOTER` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | `PLAY`, `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The menu's entries are stacked one above the next under the title and tagline. The
highlighted entry is drawn distinctly from the others, so a player always sees
which entry confirming would take. The highlight rests on the first entry when the
game opens and on every return from a game, where `PLAY` is the entry that led
away; a return from `howto` puts the highlight back on `HOW TO PLAY`, the entry
that opened that screen.

| Entry | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `PLAY` | Opens a new game, as `specs/progression.md` states, and moves to `playing`. |
| `HOW TO PLAY` | Moves to `howto`. |

Leaving the screen does nothing here. The star and a few dimmed drifting rocks may
show behind the menu if that suits the look you design.

### `howto`

How to play, written in a player's words rather than as rules of a system. It
covers:

- the goal, which is to shoot every rock down through its fragments to clear a
  wave;
- that the star pulls bullets and rocks but never the ship, so shots bend around
  it;
- that a rock the star swallows comes back from the edge of the field;
- that the saucer arrives to hunt and can be shot down;
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
- that larger rocks are armored and take several hits to break, showing their
  damage as they take them;
- that the torpedo is a single guided munition on a ten-second recharge which
  homes onto the nearest body in a narrow forward cone, flies true through the
  well, and destroys any rock outright;
{{/if}}
- the controls.

{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
The controls it names include every key `specs/controls.md` binds, written as the
standalone words `ARROWS`, `WASD`, `SPACE`, `ENTER`, `ESC`, `P`, `M`, and `F`.
{{else}}
The controls it names include every key `specs/controls.md` binds, written as the
standalone words `ARROWS`, `WASD`, `SPACE`, `ENTER`, `ESC`, `P`, and `M`.
{{/if}}

`howto` shows no menu of its own, and the title's highlight is left exactly as it
was while this screen shows. Confirming leaves the screen as leaving it does, and
both return to `title`, so a screen opened from `HOW TO PLAY` returns with that
entry highlighted.

### `playing`

{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
The live field: the star, the ship, every rock, every shot and every torpedo in
flight, the saucer while it is visiting, and every saucer bullet, with the HUD
drawn over them.
{{else}}
The live field: the star, the ship, every rock, every shot in flight, the saucer
while it is visiting, and every saucer bullet, with the HUD drawn over them.
{{/if}}

### `paused`

Reached by pausing during live play. The field stays visible behind the menu and
is frozen: nothing advances. No body moves, no timer runs down, no wave arrives,
no rock spawns, no saucer arrives or fires, and no cue plays, so a paused game is
exactly where it was when it was paused. The accumulated simulation time goes on
rising: it counts the ticks the game ran rather than the play it ran.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Menu | `PAUSE_ITEMS` | `RESUME`, `RESTART`, `QUIT TO MENU`, in that order |

| Entry | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `RESUME` | Returns to `playing` with the field and the run exactly as they stood. |
| `RESTART` | Opens a new game, as `specs/progression.md` states, and moves to `playing`. |
| `QUIT TO MENU` | Returns to `title`, with the title's highlight on `PLAY`, the entry that led away into the match. |

Leaving this screen and pausing again each do what `RESUME` does, so `Escape` and
`P` alike resume the game from here.

### `gameover`

Shown on the tick the last ship is lost. It shows the final score and the wave the
game reached.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Menu | `GAMEOVER_ITEMS` | `PLAY AGAIN`, `MENU`, in that order |

| Entry | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `PLAY AGAIN` | Opens a new game and moves to `playing`. |
| `MENU` | Returns to `title`, with the title's highlight on `PLAY`, the entry that led away into the match. |

Leaving the screen does what `MENU` does.

## Pointer and touch

The menus take a mouse and touch as well as the keyboard, over the entries the
screen shows. `title`, `paused`, and `gameover` show a menu; `howto` and `playing`
show none, and nothing in this section applies to them.

Each entry occupies a rectangular hit region the build lays out.
`specs/instrumentation.md` states the reading through which the build reports an
entry's region in logical units, so the menus keep whatever layout the build chose.

| Input | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| A pointer moves onto an entry's region | The highlight becomes that entry. |
| A touch contact lands inside an entry's region, or travels onto one | The highlight becomes that entry. |
| A pointer is pressed and released inside one entry's region | The highlight becomes that entry, and that entry is confirmed. |
| A touch contact lands and lifts inside one entry's region | The highlight becomes that entry, and that entry is confirmed. |

A confirm requires both of its edges inside one entry's region: the press and its
release for a pointer, the landing and the lift for a touch contact. Two edges that
fall in different regions, and an edge that falls outside every region, confirm no
entry. The entry every confirm acts on is the highlighted one, whichever input
raised it, and it does what this file's table for that screen says it does.

The pointer and the touch contacts are read once per frame, in the same input read
as the keys, and are applied after that frame's key edges. A press and the release
that follows it may arrive on one frame, and that frame confirms. A frame carrying
a key movement edge together with a pointer or touch selection leaves the highlight
where the pointer or the contact put it, and a frame carrying a key confirm
together with a pointer or touch confirm confirms the key's entry alone. On
`paused`, leaving the screen and pausing again are read before all of it, as
`specs/controls.md` states, so a frame carrying either resumes and does nothing
else.

## The HUD

The HUD is drawn over the `playing` screen, in the upper portion of the field and
clear of the field's centre.

| Readout | Shows |
| --- | --- |
| Score | The running score, as digits, at the top left. It is the most prominent of the readouts. |
| Ships | One small ship glyph per ship in reserve, in a row below the score. That is one fewer than the total the run has left, since the ship being flown is on the field rather than in the row, so a game that has just started draws two glyphs and a game on its last ship draws none. |
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
| Torpedo | A torpedo glyph with a charge bar beneath it, below the ships. The bar is full and the glyph lit while a torpedo is ready; while the charge is rising the glyph is dimmed and the bar fills smoothly from empty to full across the recharge. |
{{/if}}

Nothing else is required on the HUD, and a further readout of your own is welcome
there.

## The wave banner

The `WAVE N` banner is drawn centred on the field while it runs, naming the wave
about to start, as `specs/progression.md` states. It is not part of the persistent
HUD, and nothing of it is drawn once the banner has run out.
